From: Armada <noreply@armada.local>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: sm8550: add a PCIe suspend OPP

Give pcie0 a suspend-only OPP so pcie-qcom keeps a nonzero DDR/LLCC
sleep-set contract once the CPU cluster collapses in s2idle. Without it
pcie-qcom votes average/peak bandwidth of 0/0 on the PCIe memory path
during suspend; after the CPU cluster collapses, the SM8550 AOP fails
to return control and the device hard resets on wake.

Use a 1000 kBps floor. The driver's non-OPP suspend path uses
kBps_to_icc(1), a 1 kBps sleep-set peak was validated as sufficient,
and the vendor kernel votes 800 kBps for PCIe during suspend. The
selected floor is of the same order and three orders of magnitude
below the 1969000 kBps active peak.

pcie1 is disabled in the SM8550 board DTS files that use this table,
so it never probes.

The root-cause analysis and the opp-suspend approach come from Luke
Johnson's fix for this hardware in thorch
(packages/linux-thorch/patches/0221-arm64-dts-qcom-sm8550-mark-pcie-suspend-opp.patch);
this DT entry and its floor value were derived independently for
Linux 7.2.
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8550.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8550.dtsi
@@ -2444,6 +2444,19 @@
 					opp-peak-kBps = <1969000 1>;
 					opp-level = <3>;
 				};
+
+				/*
+				 * Suspend-only DDR/LLCC floor for the RPMh sleep
+				 * set. opp-hz is synthetic and opp-level is
+				 * absent, so link-speed and power-domain-level
+				 * matching cannot select it for an active link.
+				 */
+				opp-suspend-1 {
+					opp-hz = /bits/ 64 <1>;
+					required-opps = <&rpmhpd_opp_min_svs>;
+					opp-peak-kBps = <1000 1>;
+					opp-suspend;
+				};
 			};
 
 			pcieport0: pcie@0 {
